---
import LegalLayout from "../layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
import { sources } from "../lib/sources";

const preferred = [
  {
    name: "Diário da República",
    url: "https://diariodarepublica.pt/",
    used: "Portuguese law as enacted — the text itself, not a report of it.",
  },
  {
    name: "Infraestruturas de Portugal and PT Tolls",
    url: "https://www.pttolls.com/en/",
    used: "The road network, toll systems and the payment products available to visitors.",
  },
  {
    name: "IMT — Instituto da Mobilidade e dos Transportes",
    url: "https://www.imt-ip.pt/",
    used: "Driving licences, international driving permits and vehicle regulation.",
  },
  {
    name: "ANSR — Autoridade Nacional de Segurança Rodoviária",
    url: "https://ansr.pt/",
    used: "The Código da Estrada and road-safety rules.",
  },
  {
    name: "ANA Aeroportos / VINCI Airports",
    url: "https://www.ana.pt/",
    used: "Airport facilities, including the official rental-company directories.",
  },
  {
    name: "Your Europe (European Commission)",
    url: "https://europa.eu/youreurope/",
    used: "Cross-border consumer and licence questions inside the EU.",
  },
  {
    name: "A named rental company's own current terms",
    url: "",
    used: "Any claim about that specific company's deposits, cards, fuel, tolls or cross-border policy.",
  },
];
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Editorial and Research Policy | TripAxle"
  heading="Editorial and research policy"
  description="How TripAxle researches, sources and re-checks its Portugal car rental guides, what it refuses to publish, and how to send a correction."
  updated="2026-09-03"
>
  <p class="lede">
    This is the method behind every guide on the site. It exists so you can
    judge the guidance rather than take it on trust.
  </p>

  <h2>What we publish and what we refuse to</h2>
  <p>
    TripAxle explains how renting and driving a car in Portugal works. It does
    not rank rental companies, score them, name a winner, or publish
    comparisons it cannot evidence. There are no invented prices, no invented
    deposit amounts, no invented supplier policies, and no first-hand testing
    claimed that did not happen.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where a fact is genuinely variable — minimum rental age, card acceptance,
    toll service fees — we say that it varies and tell you where to find the
    answer for your own booking. That is less satisfying than a number, and it
    is the honest answer.
  </p>

  <h2>The source hierarchy</h2>
  <p>
    Guides are built from primary sources, in this order of preference. A
    competitor article, a forum thread or an aggregator page is never treated as
    evidence.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption>Sources TripAxle relies on, and what each is used for.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Source</th><th scope="col">Used for</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {preferred.map((p) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">
              {p.url ? <a href={p.url} rel="noopener">{p.name}</a> : p.name}
            </th>
            <td>{p.used}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Dates, and what each one means</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Updated</strong> — when the page's content last changed. It appears
      near the top of every guide.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Last verified</strong> — when the time-sensitive claims on the page
      were last checked against the sources listed at the foot of it. This can be
      later than the last content edit, and on a factual guide it is the date
      that matters.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Checked</strong> — shown beside each individual source: the day
      that specific document was opened and read.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A guide making time-sensitive claims is not published without a
    verification date and at least one page-specific source. That rule is
    enforced by an automated test, not by memory.
  </p>

  <h2>How we cite</h2>
  <p>
    Sources appear as ordinary crawlable links with human-readable labels — the
    publisher and the document title — never a bare URL. Authoritative citations
    are not marked <code>nofollow</code>; we want you to follow them. We
    paraphrase and attribute rather than reproducing source prose.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where a claim rests on legislation, we cite the enacted text and name the
    article, so you can check the wording yourself rather than checking our
    summary of it.
  </p>

  <h2>What we do not source, and why</h2>
  <p>
    Some things cannot be sourced responsibly, and we would rather say so than
    fill the gap:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Average rental prices.</strong> Too seasonal and too
      supplier-specific to state without misleading you.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Supplier deposit and fee amounts.</strong> These live in a
      contract that changes without notice. We tell you which clause to open.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A full current map of tolled roads.</strong> We publish the roads
      a specific law removed tolls from, sourced to that law, and point at the
      road operator's own map for everything else.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where a source is older than the page</h2>
  <p>
    Occasionally a guide is updated while one of its sources cannot be
    re-checked in the same pass. The airport rental-desk and shuttle listings
    are the standing example: they are compiled from the ANA airport
    directories, and those sites are not always readable in an automated
    verification run.
  </p>
  <p>
    When that happens we do not quietly restamp the source with today's date,
    and we do not delete the material either. The source keeps the date it was
    actually last read, the guide says so in its own words, and the official
    directory is linked so you can confirm the current position yourself. A
    listing with an honest older date is more useful than no listing — and far
    more useful than a fresh date that was never earned.
  </p>

  <h2>Independence and money</h2>
  <p>
    TripAxle is a commercial publication and may carry advertising, affiliate
    links and labelled sponsorship. No advertiser or partner writes, reviews,
    approves or influences the guidance, and nothing can be bought that changes
    a conclusion. The full terms are in the
    <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If something here is wrong or has gone out of date, tell us and it gets
    fixed. When a correction changes a factual claim, the page's updated and
    verified dates change with it, and the source list is re-checked at the same
    time. Use the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> to send one.
  </p>
  <p>
    The full citation library used across the site currently holds
    {Object.keys(sources).length} primary sources; each guide lists the subset
    it actually relies on at the foot of the page.
  </p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <p>
    TripAxle is editorial guidance, not legal, financial or insurance advice.
    Your rental agreement governs your rental, and Portuguese law governs the
    road. Where those disagree with anything here, they win. See the
    <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
